        <wgl-paragraph data-vue-component="wgl-paragraph" text="<p>The distribution of the population by migration status is not spread evenly among the cantons. At national level, 41% of the Swiss permanent resident population aged 15 or over has a migration background. This percentage is higher than 65% in the canton of Geneva. The rates are also particularly high in the cantons of Basel-Stadt, Ticino and Vaud (50% and above). In contrast, the percentage of the population with a migration background is less than 20% in the cantons of Uri and Appenzell Innerrhoden (these results should, however, be interpreted with caution as the extrapolations are based on fewer than 50 observations).&nbsp;</p>"></wgl-paragraph>






        <wgl-paragraph data-vue-component="wgl-paragraph" text="<p>The proportions of people from the first and second generation differ between cantons. At the national level, the first generation represents 33% of the permanent resident population aged 15 and over; the second generation 8%. The ratio is, therefore, around 4:1. In certain cantons such as Uri and Nidwalden, the ratio shows that the second generation is proportionally under-represented (ratio higher than 6:1). In contrast, the ratio is lower than 3:1 in the canton of Solothurn.<b></b></p>"></wgl-paragraph>







